A game has n rooms and m tunnels between them. Each room has a certain number of coins. What is the maximum number of coins you can collect while moving through the tunnels when you can freely choose your starting and ending room?

Input:
The first input line has two integers n and m: the number of rooms and tunnels. The rooms are numbered 1,2,...,n.
Then, there are n integers k_1,k_2,...,k_n: the number of coins in each room.
Finally, there are m lines describing the tunnels. Each line has two integers a and b: there is a tunnel from room a to room b. Each tunnel is a one-way tunnel.

Output:
Print one integer: the maximum number of coins you can collect.

Constraints:
1<=n<=10^5
1<=m<=2 \cdot 10^5
1<=k_i<=10^9
1<=a,b<=n

Strategy:
Here we need to put kosaraju, flattening to a DAG and SCCs all together. First we use kosaraju to find the strongly connected components and number them this gives us a DAG where a node is the SCC and the edges are the connections between them. These edges are defined by the components not having the same id. Then we find a topological ordering of this DAG since this way we make sure that we have visited all possible nodes that could lead to a node (SCC) before visiting a child. This maximizes the number of coins and allows us to run DP. In the dp we just use a max function and for each neighbour we make its state be max(dp[neigh], dp[parent]+money[neigh]) here all of the elements are the SCCs so before running this we also run a for loop to sum up the values of each of the SCCs into a total that we can use. The maximum value we find during the dp is the correct output. We also initialize each component with its own internal money in the dp. Also, kosaraju does sort into topo order automatically since thats what the whole stack thing and post order is for but i forgot so theres a topo sort.